item.text:
I used the local structured prior and did not re-fetch the structured series you already supplied. This chapter’s 2022 record is dominated by recurring custody abuse/impunity, domestic-violence protection failures, and aggressive policing of LGBT and opposition activity, with one important counterweight: a 2022 legal tightening on violence against women.

**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5"]}

**New evidence**
- Human Rights Watch’s 2023 Turkey chapter gives the broad custody-abuse baseline for the period covering 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Turkey","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/turkey","claim":"There were regular reports of torture and ill-treatment in police custody and prison, including severe beatings and cruel, inhuman, and degrading treatment, and prosecutors rarely investigated or prosecuted perpetrators.","locator":"\"Torture and Ill-Treatment in Custody\" section","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporary rights report synthesizing multiple cases and official inaction; strong for pattern evidence though not a court finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective over 2022 and the surrounding period; useful for the 2022 domestic-safety environment.","ruler_attribution":"Reflects institutions under Erdoğan in 2022, though it does not attribute every abuse to a direct presidential order.","contrary_evidence":["The report notes some isolated investigations or trials, but says they were uncommon.","Some incidents are reported rather than adjudicated."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}
- Human Rights Watch’s domestic-violence report is the strongest single source on protection failure for women in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Combatting Domestic Violence in Turkey: The Deadly Impact of Failure to Protect","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/report/2022/05/26/combatting-domestic-violence-turkey/deadly-impact-failure-protect","claim":"Failure to enforce court protection orders leaves women open to continuing abuse from current or former husbands and partners, and some women were killed despite having obtained restraining orders intended to protect them.","locator":"Summary / opening paragraphs","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed case-based investigation using complaints, court records, interviews, and 18 case files.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2019-2022 cases analyzed and published during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributes the protection gap to state institutions operating under Erdoğan's government, not to private abuse alone.","contrary_evidence":["The report acknowledges protective orders exist in law.","It does not claim every domestic-violence case was politically directed from the center."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}
- The companion HRW video/article gives the scale point and shows how many killings happened despite formal protection.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Fails Domestic Violence Victims","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/26/turkey-fails-domestic-violence-victims","claim":"Turkey's Interior Ministry estimated 307 women were killed in 2021 by men, and 38 had restraining orders issued for their protection at the time they were killed.","locator":"video transcript / opening narration","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"HRW cites official Turkish data and uses it in a contemporary rights presentation; strong for scale and failure-to-protect context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Uses 2021 baseline but was published in 2022 and directly informs the 2022 domestic-safety environment.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the protection problem persisted into Erdoğan's 2022 period, even if the killings themselves were committed by private perpetrators.","contrary_evidence":["The figures are 2021 data, not a 2022-only count.","It is a rights-advocacy presentation rather than a government audit."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.7","3B.10"]}
- The government did take a limited corrective step in 2022 by increasing penalties for violence against women.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Penalties Against the Acts of Violence Have Been Increased","publisher":"Republic of Türkiye Ministry of Justice","publication_date":"2022-05-12","url":"https://basin.adalet.gov.tr/kadina-ve-saglik-calisanlarina-siddetle-ilgili-degisiklikleri-iceren-kanun-teklifi-tbmm-de-kabul-edi","claim":"The law increased punishments for intentional killing, injury, threat, torture, and torment when committed against women and limited discretionary sentence reductions.","locator":"headline and opening summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statement about enacted legal changes in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is a ruler-era legal reform, though the Ministry frames it as a legislative measure rather than a personal act by Erdoğan alone.","contrary_evidence":["The same evidence set shows weak enforcement and recurring protection failures.","A stronger statute does not prove effective implementation."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}
- HRW documented police violence and mass arrests at Istanbul Pride, including journalists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey: Mass Arrests, Anti-LGBT Violence at Pride","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/06/30/turkey-mass-arrests-anti-lgbt-violence-pride","claim":"Turkish police attacked and arrested hundreds of people at the Istanbul Pride March on June 26, 2022, and also arrested journalists reporting on the event.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights reporting with specific counts, dates, and event details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Turkish police under Erdoğan's government; the immediate street action was local police conduct, not an abstract national trend.","contrary_evidence":["Local officials cited security and public-order reasons.","The report is rights-focused and not a full policing audit."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.3","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- Amnesty adds a concrete example from the METU campus, showing the same pattern of force against peaceful assembly.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: Further information: Pride march dispersed with excessive force","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-06-23","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur44/5746/2022/en/","claim":"Police violently broke up the student-organized Pride march at Middle East Technical University in Ankara on 10 June, detaining 37 students after dragging some of them on the ground.","locator":"opening summary","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous human-rights reporting on a specific event with a clear date and casualty/detention count.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The conduct is attributed to Turkish police and the authorities allowing the crackdown; it does not show a presidential order by itself.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities framed the event as a public-order issue.","The incident is a specific protest crackdown, not proof of nationwide mass terror."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.8","3B.7"]}
- Reuters reported a broader Istanbul Pride ban and detentions after authorities blocked the march.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish police prevent Istanbul Pride from going ahead","publisher":"Reuters via Openly","publication_date":"2022-06-27","url":"https://www.openlynews.com/i/?id=6ea4c88e-ac89-40e8-a29c-6db374da4987","claim":"Turkish police blocked hundreds of people from gathering for Istanbul's annual Pride parade and detained dozens after local authorities banned the march.","locator":"lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters dispatch is strong for event reporting and matches the HRW/Amnesty accounts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the state environment under Erdoğan, though the operational ban was issued by local authorities and carried out by police.","contrary_evidence":["Local authorities said the march was banned for security reasons.","The piece reports the crackdown, not the full legal basis."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- Erdoğan’s own public rhetoric in late 2022 was explicitly hostile to LGBT people.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan says constitutional change will protect families against 'perverse trends'","publisher":"Reuters via Yahoo News","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/erdogan-says-constitutional-change-protect-154751055.html","claim":"Erdogan said a constitutional amendment would protect families from what he called 'perverse trends' and said he wanted to 'protect the family establishment' against LGBT-related change.","locator":"direct quotation in article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a direct Erdoğan quotation; useful for ruler attribution even though republished by Yahoo.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Erdoğan, not merely to subordinates or local police.","contrary_evidence":["The speech was framed as family-protection politics by Erdoğan and AKP supporters.","It is rhetoric, not proof of a specific violent act."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.7"]}
- The new disinformation law was quickly used against a journalist.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In a first, Turkish court arrests journalist under 'disinformation' law","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/in-a-first-turkish-court-arrests-journalist-under-disinformation-law-2965043","claim":"A Turkish court ordered the arrest of journalist Sinan Aygul in the first pre-trial detention under a new law that critics said could be used to stifle dissent.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters fast-moving legal reporting with a concrete first-use claim and a named defendant.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the legal climate under Erdoğan's governing party; the arrest itself was made by the judiciary/police, not personally by the president.","contrary_evidence":["The government said the law was meant to protect the public.","This is a free-speech/detention case more than a direct physical-violence case."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- Kurdish journalists were held in custody on propaganda charges, again showing the security apparatus’s use of detention.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye’de 15 gazeteci ve 1 medya çalışanı tutuklandı","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-06-17","url":"https://cpj.org/tr/2022/06/turkiyede-15-gazeteci-ve-1-medya-calisani-tutuklandi/","claim":"A Turkish court imprisoned pending trial 16 Kurdish journalists and media workers after they were detained on accusations of spreading terrorist propaganda, with no formal charges made available during the detention period.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CPJ is a specialist press-freedom monitor and the account is contemporaneous and specific.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Turkish courts, prosecutors, and police operating under Erdoğan's government; the arrests themselves were not a presidential order in the evidence cited.","contrary_evidence":["The authorities portrayed the case as anti-terror enforcement.","The article is press-freedom focused and not a general domestic-safety survey."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.8"]}
- The opposition was also hit by selective judicial punishment that fed a fear climate.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish court sentences Erdogan rival to jail with political ban","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/turkish-court-orders-jail-political-ban-for-erdogan-rival-2964503","claim":"A Turkish court sentenced Istanbul Mayor Ekrem Imamoglu to jail and imposed a political ban on the opposition politician seen as a strong challenger to President Tayyip Erdogan.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters event reporting with direct political consequence and a clear date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The court action was not personally ordered by Erdoğan in the source, but it is relevant to the political-fear environment under his rule.","contrary_evidence":["The judgment was subject to appeal.","This is political punishment rather than direct physical violence."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- Erdogan’s 2022 anti-LGBT and family rhetoric was tied, in Reuters reporting, to planned constitutional and legal moves.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan moots putting Turkey headscarf reform to referendum","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/03/turkey-politics-headscarf","claim":"Reuters reported that critics saw Erdogan's constitutional-amendment push as targeting the LGBT+ community, and the article quoted him saying he aimed to protect the family from 'perverse trends'.","locator":"middle paragraphs on critics and quoted speech","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting ties the public speech to contemporaneous political effects and to the planned amendment debate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Erdoğan rhetoric, with the caveat that the constitutional change was still a proposal.","contrary_evidence":["Supporters framed the proposal as protecting headscarf rights.","The link to LGBT targeting is an interpretive claim by critics, not an official admission."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.7","3B.8"]}
- The Istanbul bombing aftermath shows the domestic security climate and the state’s immediate response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey blames Istanbul blast on Kurdish militants, arrests 22, including bomber","publisher":"Reuters via ThePrint","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://theprint.in/world/turkey-blames-istanbul-blast-on-kurdish-militants-arrests-22-including-bomber/1215168/","claim":"Turkey's interior minister said the blast on Istanbul's Istiklal Avenue killed six people and that police had detained 22 suspects, including the person who planted the bomb.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters dispatch with concrete casualty and detention details about a major domestic security event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Turkish state response under Erdoğan's government, though the immediate operational response ran through the interior ministry and police.","contrary_evidence":["The source reports the government's claim about blame, not an independent forensic finding.","This is a crisis-response event, not a full accountability record."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 25-40 documents across HRW, Amnesty, CPJ, Reuters syndications, Turkish official legal pages, and a few broader background items.
- I kept sources that added distinct value on one of four themes: custody abuse/impunity, domestic violence protection failures, LGBT/protest policing, and selective legal punishment or crisis response.
- I rejected duplicate Reuters syndications of the same story when they added no new claim, 2021-only background unless it clearly carried forward into 2022, and items with no direct physical-safety nexus.
- Some direct Reuters pages were inaccessible in this environment, so I used Reuters-republished copies in Yahoo, Euronews, Openly, Investing, and ThePrint where the Reuters text itself was visible.
- I did not use the client matrix or re-fetch the structured local sources you already provided.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have a direct 2022 source showing Erdoğan personally ordered or reviewed specific police-abuse cases, so attribution remains institutional rather than command-specific for some detention abuses.
- The domestic-violence record is strong on failure to protect, but weaker on exact high-level responsibility chains between presidential office, ministries, police, and courts.
- The Istanbul blast source gives crisis-response context, but not a full independent investigation of proportionality, legality, or follow-up accountability.
- If you want a finer 3B dossier, the next best gain would come from Turkish-language legal reporting on protest bans, custody abuse, and women’s-protection enforcement in late 2022.
